1
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:03,200
Welcome to Launched.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:10,480
I'm Charlie Chapman, and today I'm excited to bring you the creator of the Apple Design Award winning iOS game Blackbox, Ryan McCloud.

3
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:25,440
Ryan, thanks for joining me.

4
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:26,400
Thanks for having me.

5
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:28,640
I'm super excited about this.

6
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:33,040
I think you're the first game developer I've had on this show.

7
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:39,760
And so there's a bunch of interesting things I've always wondered about this as someone who's never, uh, never published a game or made one.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,080
So, uh, I'm really interested to see how this goes.

9
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:43,040
Spice it up.

10
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:44,400
I don't know.

11
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:46,160
Yeah, I guess I'm a game developer.

12
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:46,880
It's true.

13
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,720
Yeah, I think that's accurate.

14
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:49,760
That's true.

15
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:54,240
But before we get into Blackbox, uh, I'd like to give everyone a background on who you are.

16
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:57,360
So the three questions I ask everyone are where you're from?

17
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,160
Do you have a formal education related to what you do?

18
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:04,800
And then, what was your career like leading up to the release of Blackbox?

19
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:05,760
Sure.

20
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,880
Yeah, I think those all roll together pretty nicely.

21
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:12,880
Yeah, I was born and raised in San Diego in California.

22
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:29,680
And then I went to school at Cal Poly in San Luis Obispo, like a state technical school, the computer science program, which I had applied to different schools as computer science or computer engineering just because I liked computers.

23
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:35,920
And then happened to go there and found out that I picked computer science, which ended up being fun.

24
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:37,520
Well, that's good.

25
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:37,840
Yeah.

26
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,840
Have no formal design background.

27
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:46,480
So that was kind of more left to my own devices.

28
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:48,720
But I graduated.

29
00:01:49,600 --> 00:02:08,880
and I was kind of I had done web development work before in various internships and stuff, but had always been really fascinated by the iPhone since day one, as I'm sure a lot of people were, and really liked this idea of like being able to create novel interfaces and such.

30
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:14,720
And I think there was always a frustration of like wanting to make my own things with that.

31
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:23,360
And that eventually led to me experimenting with iOS when I got kind of tired of web development.

32
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:28,000
And I think I looked at a lot of jobs that I thought would be cool.

33
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:32,960
And like all of these jobs kind of asked as a requirement, like what's an app that you have in the app store?

34
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:33,680
Yeah.

35
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:38,800
And I thought, okay, I might need to do that.

36
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:44,880
And that's kind of where Blackbox came from, really, long story short.

37
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:54,320
But I was messing around with this, this idea of this like mystery box, that kind of wouldn't have any visuals or any hints.

38
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:59,200
It's just like all these lights that you would like, every black box challenge running at once was the original idea.

39
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:00,800
Kind of chaotic.

40
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:02,080
Yeah, yeah.

41
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:04,560
It would be difficult to scale.

42
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:05,840
Yeah, yeah.

43
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:17,120
I think I didn't understand how the hardware worked at that point, but I eventually made something that friends would like, I would hand it to them and they wouldn't give me the phone back super easily.

44
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:20,320
And I kind of realized like maybe I could really turn this into a game.

45
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:23,120
And there was kind of some indie inspirations out there.

46
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:29,200
And I kind of read up on it and thought it might be possible, but like, I could tell that it was definitely hard.

47
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:34,800
So I just decided to give it the best I could and then have it out there.

48
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:36,880
And that could always serve as kind of a portfolio.

49
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:39,120
piece to get a real job.

50
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:41,680
And that hasn't happened.

51
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:42,000
Yeah.

52
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:44,480
So here we are.

53
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:50,080
So the initial thing was you wanted to have an app in the store that you could use as a portfolio piece to get a job.

54
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:54,080
What led you down the route of wanting to make a game?

55
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:58,240
Was it just because you were experimenting with all this sensor stuff?

56
00:03:58,960 --> 00:03:59,040
Yeah.

57
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:09,040
It's interesting because I think like when I was a kid, a lot of people probably had this like loved games, wanted to make games, tried to figure out how to make games.

58
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:17,520
You know, I like like got a book on C plus plus because I found out that that's what StarCraft was made in, like, never figured out how to compute or run C plus plus.

59
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:17,840
Yeah.

60
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:20,320
Like, I've that's what a ten year old is going to do.

61
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:21,280
I mean, now they do.

62
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:22,560
They're much smarter.

63
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:27,680
But yeah, it wasn't really, I guess, I don't know.

64
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:31,440
I did have there's a distinction of like puzzle versus game.

65
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:32,400
I never thought of it.

66
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:33,840
It was clearly a game.

67
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:43,680
But I remember very early getting invited by another indie game developer to go to the game developers conference in San Francisco, like, shortly after launch.

68
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:47,040
And I kind of felt like, I'm not a game developer.

69
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:57,120
It took a long time to kind of come around to that because I still identify more as being an app developer and like, Blackbox is entirely made in UI kit and stuff like that.

70
00:04:57,680 --> 00:04:59,840
Not really an answer, but No, that makes sense.

71
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:18,960
Like, it and it kind of makes sense for why you we haven't explained what Blackbox is, but essentially it's a puzzle game that what makes it incredibly unique, apart from really cool art, you know, design and everything, is it somehow uses every possible sensor available on an iPhone and makes puzzles out of them.

72
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:19,680
Almost.

73
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:20,320
Almost.

74
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:20,480
Okay.

75
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:32,640
And so that angle is something that someone who's really familiar with, you know, Unity or a quote unquote game platform.

76
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:33,600
Right.

77
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:35,200
would probably be a lot more difficult, right?

78
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:38,000
You'd have to build a whole bunch of shims to connect all that together.

79
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:38,480
Yeah.

80
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:45,120
And so it kind of makes sense that you coming at it from that angle is part of what made this such a unique feeling experience.

81
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:46,960
Definitely agree with that.

82
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:54,080
I think early on, that was sort of where some of the inspiration came from.

83
00:05:54,720 --> 00:06:00,320
Because that was around the time Flappy Bird was coming, becoming popular and kind of these like Endless Runner type things.

84
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:00,800
Yeah.

85
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:26,320
And I don't mean to be mean to an entire genre of games, but I felt like everything was just kind of dumbing down almost in this, like getting simpler and simpler and like not like dumbing down, but like using less of the hardware and making like these more portable experiences and these like lowest common denominator type things where it's like this can be played on any glass rectangle that has touch.

86
00:06:26,880 --> 00:06:27,040
Right.

87
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:32,160
Which kind of feels like that's what, you know, app development in general was sort of doing around that time too, right?

88
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:39,600
Like Facebook Mobile was the simplified version of Facebook for if you're on your phone, but like the full experience would be on desktop.

89
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:45,040
And that felt like that was kind of across the ecosystem, including games, I guess, a similar trend.

90
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:45,680
For sure.

91
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:46,080
Yeah.

92
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:49,200
I think there was a lot like coming from other platforms.

93
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:56,080
And there had always been things that were born on mobile, but that didn't seem to be a priority anymore.

94
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:57,600
Like people had fun in the early years.

95
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:00,880
And then it was kind of like, okay, that doesn't make a ton of business sense anymore.

96
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:02,240
We need to get this out the door.

97
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:18,480
And I found that like really a huge shame because there's so much cool stuff to do with these platforms and to use these sorts of sensors and things as context to like make invisible design and things like that, like a lot better.

98
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:33,040
There were a lot of early apps that were like really inspirational in that way and like playing around, you know, using the proximity sensor to try to I think it was like Peak tried to do that, but I think IDEO did that.

99
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:37,680
It was like you could cover the top of the phone to see something, like a different view.

100
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:42,960
I think they ended up using the camera because the proximity sensor turns off the screen, but like fun stuff like that.

101
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:59,360
It makes me think of kind of the difference between like a traditional AAA game developer and Nintendo, where Nintendo likes to take whatever hardware they put out and do the most, like stretch it as far as they can in a way that makes it almost impossible to port to something else.

102
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:01,520
But that's part of what its charm is.

103
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:02,000
Totally.

104
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:19,120
And I imagine, I'm sure you get requests all the time for an Android version of Blackbox, but every time I think of that, I'm like, that's kind of borderline impossible because you'd have to target a specific Android phone with known sensors, you know, to build all these special experiences, right?

105
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:27,680
Yeah, it's always that one's always kind of heartbreaking because so many people have this concept concept conception that like I haven't pressed the Android release button.

106
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:38,720
It's just like it was that goat simulator that released an update and their trailer for it just showed them opening Unity and saying file, you know, new update or something.

107
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:40,480
And they hit that and then it just said ship.

108
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:43,200
Like, yeah, that's how a lot of people view these things, I think.

109
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:45,520
And now it's on PlayStation 5.

110
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:45,760
Yeah.

111
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:47,520
Yeah.

112
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:49,840
I never know how much of this to explain to people.

113
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:56,000
So I always try to like lay out a little bit of it just so they know I'm not, uh, total, uh, yeah, we'll keep it clean.

114
00:08:56,480 --> 00:08:57,360
It's, uh, yeah.

115
00:08:57,360 --> 00:08:59,520
I mean, I've had problems with iOS hardware even.

116
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:08,400
This hasn't happened in a long time, but like the iPhone 6S, like changed the audio processing hardware and it changed like microphone bit rates and stuff like that.

117
00:09:08,560 --> 00:09:09,920
And that was a total pain to fix.

118
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:11,760
So I can't imagine that.

119
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:12,560
I imagine.

120
00:09:13,040 --> 00:09:15,920
At scale among a dozen other big issues.

121
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:28,160
But yeah, it's very tailored to iOS and I think the point about Nintendo is interesting and fun because like within those constraints is born a lot of freedom and interesting things.

122
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:33,280
You know, it's kind of like Lego is really good at that where they don't create new parts really easily.

123
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:36,160
You know, you have to dig in the bucket you already have to come up with stuff.

124
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:38,640
And leads to some interesting things.

125
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:42,240
So what, what time, like, period was this?

126
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:45,200
Like, what version of the iPhone are we talking about when you started building this?

127
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:51,600
I think I like, the first commit is actually somewhere around like 2014, late.

128
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:51,840
Okay.

129
00:09:52,320 --> 00:09:54,640
That year, but I wasn't going at it super hard then.

130
00:09:56,320 --> 00:10:01,360
It was more 2016 and yeah, or 2015 and then launched in 2016.

131
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:06,000
I think that was like the iPhone 5S, maybe 4S.

132
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:06,640
Okay.

133
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:08,880
Just I was trying to get a frame of reference for what.

134
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:09,280
Yeah.

135
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:10,880
How many devices at that time?

136
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:18,960
Because now we're in a world where where there's a lot of fragmentation even within the iPhone, which I imagine makes your job a lot harder.

137
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:22,160
Yes, there were multiple screen sizes.

138
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:23,600
Okay, at that point.

139
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:31,200
That's probably good because if you had made it for a single screen size, I imagine the foundations would have been pretty difficult to translate.

140
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:34,000
Yeah, yeah, I moved to autolayout pretty quickly.

141
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:50,080
So at what point did you start treating this like this isn't just a portfolio thing that I may or may not release and start treating it like this is a real product and I'm going to really put all my, you know, and maybe marketing power behind it or did that happen after release?

142
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:56,080
I think it was like it was something I thought about and was like meditated.

143
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:06,800
I I read a ton on everything I could find on other indie developers and publishing things and how the gold rush was over and how marketing is important and indies don't pay attention to it.

144
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:15,520
And I kind of zeroed in on that I needed to be featured by Apple probably to be successful.

145
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:22,880
And I did sort of I had a lot of things thoughts in mind from like using other apps how I wanted to make it.

146
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:24,000
I wanted to make it work.

147
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:29,360
I did want to have some sort of purchase in it to try to make it something I wasn't going to give away completely for free.

148
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:33,360
And there was a lot of thought behind that for launch.

149
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:45,440
But then at launch, it ended up getting featured in a very small way and enough that it was picked up by some other reviewers and YouTubers and stuff.

150
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:50,480
And a lot of friends were really stoked and I was really stoked as well.

151
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:57,920
But I kind of, I think I knew a little bit more and thought, like, I don't know if these are sustainable numbers or if this can really be a business.

152
00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:04,400
But within those first few weeks, it was like, oh my God, this is going to change my life sort of thing.

153
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:06,240
You have that graph of launch.

154
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:07,040
Right.

155
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:09,120
I had no experience with how that goes after.

156
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:13,600
And it like very quickly sunk down pretty low.

157
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:20,480
And I think I did have this idea that like I put all this work into this thing and I put all the marketing I can behind it and launch it.

158
00:12:20,960 --> 00:12:22,240
And then I will buy a boat.

159
00:12:22,880 --> 00:12:23,840
And that did not happen.

160
00:12:25,040 --> 00:12:30,800
So it was a very quick realization that it was kind of this ongoing thing.

161
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:41,680
I think like a few months later I had fixed some bugs and stuff, but I started getting approached by people for iOS jobs, which was like brand new to me and really exciting.

162
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:47,840
So I started interviewing at some places and I couldn't quite let it go.

163
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
I think I still really wanted to see if I could make it work.

164
00:12:50,240 --> 00:13:01,600
So I made a new puzzle pack and put in a bunch of new in-app purchase type things for hints and stuff like that and relaunched it in October of that year.

165
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:04,800
So it launched in February and then did that in October.

166
00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:08,720
Relaunched meaning like did a big marketing push around an update?

167
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:10,160
Did a big update, yeah.

168
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:10,400
Okay.

169
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:12,000
Yeah, not a real relaunch.

170
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:14,800
but put a lot more effort into it and new stuff.

171
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:16,880
And then like, saw that graph go up again.

172
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:24,480
And I kind of had this like, oh, like, this is going to be like an ongoing thing, you know?

173
00:13:24,880 --> 00:13:29,600
But like, then I kind of saw it was possible and decided to like really dive into it.

174
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:39,120
And so I guess based on what you're saying then, it sounds like from the get-go, the business model evolved into it's a free app.

175
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:44,640
Because it was free totally upfront with a certain amount of puzzles that you get no matter what.

176
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:48,640
I locked like ten puzzles out of like fifty or sixty.

177
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:50,640
50 or 60, which was a huge mistake.

178
00:13:50,880 --> 00:13:50,960
Yeah.

179
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:51,440
Wow.

180
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:55,120
And that was one in that purchase to unlock it?

181
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:57,040
It was a dollar on launch day.

182
00:13:57,600 --> 00:13:59,360
I think I made it two dollars later.

183
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:00,400
Wow.

184
00:14:01,200 --> 00:14:01,280
Okay.

185
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:01,760
Yeah.

186
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:09,040
I had this idea that people would like diehard puzzle people would get really far into the game and then they would like absolutely to buy it.

187
00:14:09,200 --> 00:14:14,560
And I didn't understand how low conversion rates on the App Store are and how people play games.

188
00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:15,360
Yeah.

189
00:14:16,240 --> 00:14:16,480
Interesting.

190
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:20,720
So did you, I mean, did you eventually like reign that in then?

191
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:21,200
Yeah.

192
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:24,000
I think I figured that out a few weeks ago.

193
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:30,560
So, it's been a slow process, but we're getting there.

194
00:14:31,200 --> 00:14:36,720
So the primary, the business model then is single purchases of new packs.

195
00:14:37,200 --> 00:14:38,800
And so you releasing a new pack.

196
00:14:39,040 --> 00:14:39,120
Yeah.

197
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:44,320
And then having to market that, presumably to try and get people to actually pay for it.

198
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:47,840
That's how you've been able to fund this long term?

199
00:14:48,560 --> 00:14:49,280
That is the idea.

200
00:14:49,680 --> 00:14:53,440
I haven't released a new pack since that October in 2017.

201
00:14:53,760 --> 00:14:53,840
Wow.

202
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:55,040
Yeah.

203
00:14:55,280 --> 00:15:02,480
I've been, I've added a few bunch of puzzles for free.

204
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:14,480
I think part of what I realized is that a lot of like the people that were supporting development and paying were the new people that were coming in the door every day.

205
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:23,280
And so kind of hurt because I just wanted to make puzzles, but I realized I needed to turn my attention towards like making the app a more optimal experience.

206
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:26,560
And so I balanced that now.

207
00:15:27,040 --> 00:15:33,280
But the it's the whole game now is basically one five dollar purchase for all the locked content.

208
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:36,000
And there's kind of a very soft paywall on that.

209
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:38,160
So you can play half the game for free.

210
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:41,600
And then I also sell like packs of hints if you need.

211
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:45,360
And now I do merch and you can donate burritos and things.

212
00:15:45,680 --> 00:15:48,400
And lots of very generous people support me that way.

213
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:52,240
But it's been possible to do it full time for the last few years.

214
00:15:52,640 --> 00:15:58,400
And that so the main driver then of continued growth has just been new players.

215
00:15:59,120 --> 00:15:59,600
Yeah, yeah.

216
00:16:00,160 --> 00:16:04,880
I'm super lucky to have like a really consistent stream of new players.

217
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:07,360
