But that just came from like people were asking for merch and I thought it was a little goofy.

427
00:32:07,840 --> 00:32:12,480
Like I printed some shirts for myself and friends early on for going to conferences and stuff.

428
00:32:12,640 --> 00:32:18,640
But then people were asking for merch and like I guess like I don't wear a ton of merch so I didn't think of this as a thing.

429
00:32:19,280 --> 00:32:24,800
Uh, and so I did something with Cotton Bureau and had like links in the app and they were really nice.

430
00:32:25,120 --> 00:32:29,280
where they let me like do a custom size tag and hide a URL in there and stuff.

431
00:32:30,800 --> 00:32:31,680
It's a fun secret.

432
00:32:32,720 --> 00:32:52,000
But then, yeah, this like a lot of fan startup called Monetizer approached me and they like after the design award and wanted to try doing something and I had all these people asking for this and it was like, if you're going to handle the like fulfillment and making everything and like it has Apple Pay, it was like, like, if I don't have to do anything, like sure.

433
00:32:53,440 --> 00:33:09,120
And it kind of grew into a fun thing where like they were super willing to let me do whatever I wanted or like they sent me like a die line for boxes and there was this area that it seemed like it was under the folds of the boxes.

434
00:33:09,200 --> 00:33:10,640
And I was like, can we print that?

435
00:33:10,800 --> 00:33:11,760
And they're like, I guess.

436
00:33:11,920 --> 00:33:12,960
Like nobody will see that.

437
00:33:13,200 --> 00:33:13,840
It's like, cool.

438
00:33:14,480 --> 00:33:14,800
Perfect.

439
00:33:16,320 --> 00:33:24,800
Like all the merchandise, it became like this fun way for me to play with physical puzzles where they all come with little riddles on the stickers or printed or hidden and stuff like that.

440
00:33:25,040 --> 00:33:26,320
And I don't know.

441
00:33:26,960 --> 00:33:27,840
It's mostly a fun thing.

442
00:33:28,000 --> 00:33:28,880
That's awesome though.

443
00:33:29,040 --> 00:33:32,400
Yeah, because you have a bunch of different things.

444
00:33:32,720 --> 00:33:33,920
It's not just shirts and stickers.

445
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:35,840
It's like these custom boxes and stuff, right?

446
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:45,600
Yeah, yeah, there's like well, there's like an email pin and there's a hat and now there's like a big box of everything, stickers, we just said white stickers, I just put some on my black bike.

447
00:33:46,960 --> 00:33:50,480
We were working on, I was working on some socks, we'll see if that happens.

448
00:33:51,680 --> 00:33:55,200
I don't know, it's a goofy fun thing, but people really like it.

449
00:33:55,680 --> 00:34:01,760
So a couple of weeks ago as we record, you released Blackbox 2.0, right?

450
00:34:02,240 --> 00:34:02,320
Yeah.

451
00:34:03,040 --> 00:34:08,000
What I wasn't completely clear what was actually 2.0 about it.

452
00:34:08,240 --> 00:34:08,640
That's fair.

453
00:34:09,040 --> 00:34:11,600
So I'm curious if you can like talk through what that is.

454
00:34:12,160 --> 00:34:17,920
Yeah.0 is kind of a big term for it, like externally.

455
00:34:18,480 --> 00:34:20,640
For me internally, it felt more like that.

456
00:34:22,400 --> 00:34:25,600
These days, of course, we just release updates all the time.

457
00:34:26,160 --> 00:34:36,400
And it's kind of fun to like try to batch some stuff up, but then it's it'll feel bad where it's like you're working on something so long and like people would really enjoy this and benefit from it.

458
00:34:36,560 --> 00:34:38,640
Like why not just release it bit by bit?

459
00:34:39,920 --> 00:34:47,360
But there's been like three major things that I kind of wanted to accomplish to feel like Blackbox was more complete.

460
00:34:50,640 --> 00:34:52,800
So Blackbox is in about twenty languages now.

461
00:34:53,520 --> 00:34:55,040
And then the other one was iPad.

462
00:34:55,760 --> 00:34:59,360
And I thought I was going to finish this like three years ago, I think.

463
00:34:59,680 --> 00:35:00,080
Oh man.

464
00:35:00,480 --> 00:35:11,280
And Blackbox was pretty iPad compatible, but I'm crazy, and so I wanted to remake every single view to try to be more like iPad proper.

465
00:35:12,400 --> 00:35:19,760
And so over the last few years I've been like remaking all of these views but releasing them just on the iPhone and then doing the iPad work on the side.

466
00:35:20,160 --> 00:35:21,680
So that was the big impetus.

467
00:35:22,240 --> 00:35:24,000
And also I had reached 1.9.

468
00:35:24,400 --> 00:35:31,360
And then trolled everyone on the Discord server by releasing 1.10 and had to do a 2.0 at some point.

469
00:35:32,320 --> 00:35:39,680
So it had some other big things in there and there was supposed to be a little bit more, but I kind of got a little close to burnout on that.

470
00:35:39,840 --> 00:35:46,240
So I'm holding back on some of those like new haptics, a few of those made it in and some other fun things that will come.

471
00:35:47,040 --> 00:35:47,440
Awesome.

472
00:35:48,320 --> 00:35:54,880
I'm curious, has that felt like a bigger release in terms of the reception because of the number?

473
00:35:55,360 --> 00:35:58,000
I've always been curious about this numbering of things, right?

474
00:35:58,240 --> 00:35:59,280
Because everyone has opinions.

475
00:35:59,680 --> 00:35:59,840
Yeah.

476
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:10,320
Yeah, I think I get super self conscious all the time that like people are just like waiting on me and that like people are keeping track of the days that a bug has been out and stuff like that.

477
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:13,280
And it just like gives me a lot of like subconscious stress.

478
00:36:13,600 --> 00:36:20,800
And it was a huge relief to get so many of these little things that I had been fixing out in 2.0.

479
00:36:21,520 --> 00:36:25,600
But I was kind of worried that people would see through it and be like, You call that 2.0, you know?

480
00:36:26,480 --> 00:36:31,440
But I think it was more a lesson in like people kind of feel the way that you like drum it up.

481
00:36:31,760 --> 00:36:35,360
So I made it a big deal and everyone celebrated it as a big deal.

482
00:36:36,720 --> 00:36:46,880
Maybe they really thought that way too, but I felt relieved that it was received pretty well and everyone was still excited even though I didn't manage to get a puzzle out in that build.

483
00:36:47,680 --> 00:36:50,960
Yeah, I I mean, that's definitely the approach that I take.

484
00:36:51,360 --> 00:36:56,080
I don't know if that's the right one, but I I just like getting excited about things.

485
00:36:56,320 --> 00:37:03,520
And so I just took the approach of like every new feature basically, which is for me like once a year, I'm just going to give it a new number.

486
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:10,480
And it seems like the new number alone, people kind of get excited about it and we'll check it out again or whatever.

487
00:37:10,560 --> 00:37:12,640
And most it's not like it's not like most.

488
00:37:13,200 --> 00:37:16,480
people are following version to version, I imagine.

489
00:37:16,880 --> 00:37:17,040
Right.

490
00:37:17,440 --> 00:37:23,920
So seeing a number flip like that is enough to make a lot of people go, Oh, I wonder what's changed.

491
00:37:24,080 --> 00:37:30,800
And like they're seeing the changes since the last time they really checked it out, which maybe multiple versions of features kind of poured into it.

492
00:37:30,960 --> 00:37:38,880
I think that's a really good point, because I got a lot of emails about people checking it out again or asking how to reset it to start over, which always sounds horrifying to me.

493
00:37:41,520 --> 00:37:43,120
So I think there's a lot of that.

494
00:37:43,280 --> 00:37:46,560
And a lot, a lot has changed since 1.0.

495
00:37:46,720 --> 00:37:54,480
So if people are coming in and trying it again., it's it's really a much more polished experience and closer to like what I've always wanted it to be.

496
00:37:55,040 --> 00:38:02,880
So I guess the last big question then is, do you already have a journal full of ideas for black box AR?

497
00:38:03,600 --> 00:38:05,760
Whenever the glasses of the game show up?

498
00:38:06,960 --> 00:38:15,920
You know, yeah, it's something there's been I've looked into doing AR stuff and sort of concluded that it would have to be a completely different app and game.

499
00:38:16,240 --> 00:38:16,320
Yeah.

500
00:38:17,120 --> 00:38:26,160
I really need to learn some 3D, which I've been saying for a year or two now, but I think I'm very comfortable in UIKit.

501
00:38:26,240 --> 00:38:33,200
I still don't know how game engines really work, but that seems like a place we should be moving to and learning.

502
00:38:34,240 --> 00:39:01,200
It'll be interesting to see what Apple does because I can't imagine they're going to, if assuming that all the rumors we've been hearing for a decade are true and their next big platform they're trying to push into is these glasses or whatever, I can't imagine they're going to try and bring the huge developer ecosystem that they've built and all these developers who don't work in game engines and try and move them into a game engine only world..

503
00:39:01,600 --> 00:39:12,320
So I bet there will be a whole, you know, Swift UI based or something framework for building good UI that works in this sort of environment.

504
00:39:13,200 --> 00:39:14,000
But yeah, who knows?

505
00:39:14,400 --> 00:39:14,720
It's hard.

506
00:39:14,880 --> 00:39:16,000
It has to be 3D, right?

507
00:39:16,320 --> 00:39:19,760
It's a completely different paradigm for our rectangle world.

508
00:39:21,680 --> 00:39:23,920
Rectangle with rounded corners and notches now.

509
00:39:24,400 --> 00:39:28,240
Yeah, yeah, sorry, continuous curve and a squaggle world.

510
00:39:29,040 --> 00:39:31,760
Well, I guess we can go ahead and start wrapping up.

511
00:39:32,400 --> 00:39:36,720
I can ask you the question that I ask everybody, which is what's a person or people?

512
00:39:37,280 --> 00:39:40,240
out there that have inspired you that you would recommend other people to check out?

513
00:39:40,560 --> 00:39:43,360
There's a few, I don't know how many, how many you want here.

514
00:39:44,160 --> 00:39:44,880
As many as you want.

515
00:39:45,360 --> 00:39:46,560
I was trying to think through this.

516
00:39:47,040 --> 00:39:51,120
Well, I think two main ones that I was thinking about, a design one and the more random one.

517
00:39:51,680 --> 00:39:57,600
I really like hearing about people solving problems in other spaces.

518
00:39:58,560 --> 00:40:06,480
And Amanda Pinscher did a really good talk on designing for the command line at GitHub, their new command line app.

519
00:40:06,880 --> 00:40:12,480
And there's a lot of really interesting little points in there about like using the context in a different space like that.

520
00:40:12,800 --> 00:40:14,960
that I think are interesting to hear.

521
00:40:15,520 --> 00:40:23,680
And then more random, I was thinking, there's this Twitter account called Bees and Bombs.

522
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:24,480
Do you know it?

523
00:40:24,960 --> 00:40:25,840
Bees and Bombs?

524
00:40:26,240 --> 00:40:26,320
Yeah.

525
00:40:26,880 --> 00:40:31,360
No, well, if I do, it's from other people retweeting it or something, but I can't recall it.

526
00:40:31,600 --> 00:40:37,280
So the account, like, it's funny when you realize like you know something so intimately, but you know nothing about it, you know?

527
00:40:37,760 --> 00:40:37,840
Yeah.

528
00:40:38,960 --> 00:40:54,320
So like Bees and Bombs is the handle, and it's run by this, I presume a guy named Dave, because that's on they just make these, like, really trippy optical illusion type GIFs that loop.

529
00:40:54,880 --> 00:40:59,920
And they're, I don't know, they bring me a lot of joy because they're, I just love optical illusions, one.

530
00:41:00,240 --> 00:41:04,320
And then two, they're kind of a fun puzzle to stir at and be like, how, what's the math here?

531
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:05,440
Like, how is this repeating?

532
00:41:05,600 --> 00:41:06,640
Like, could I make this type thing?

533
00:41:06,800 --> 00:41:09,040
I think it's a great design challenge for anyone.

534
00:41:09,760 --> 00:41:22,720
I don't know what this person does or like who they are, if they have a like a full time job, but they consistently put out new, uh, crazy optical illusion GIFs like every day or every other day or something.

535
00:41:22,880 --> 00:41:23,840
I've definitely seen these.

536
00:41:24,160 --> 00:41:25,120
I retweet these a lot.

537
00:41:25,280 --> 00:41:26,240
Yeah, it's probably from you.

538
00:41:27,440 --> 00:41:28,480
Yeah, quite possibly.

539
00:41:29,040 --> 00:41:30,960
I just smash retweet all the time.

540
00:41:31,120 --> 00:41:33,840
It's just, I think it's fun art to interrupt the timeline.

541
00:41:34,400 --> 00:41:40,080
Yeah, this sort of stuff is like super inspiring for Blackbox, way beyond my skills often.

542
00:41:42,080 --> 00:41:52,080
I really recommend like checking these out and trying to, I don't know, any engineer designer, I think, would have a lot of fun pulling these apart and trying to figure out like how they loop.

543
00:41:52,400 --> 00:41:54,400
There's often a lot of little tricks.

544
00:41:54,960 --> 00:41:57,040
Yeah, this is, uh, mesmerizing.

545
00:41:57,600 --> 00:41:58,320
You're looking at them.

546
00:41:58,720 --> 00:42:00,960
Yeah, that's why I went silent and stuff for a while.

547
00:42:00,960 --> 00:42:02,400
and stop contributing to the conversation.

548
00:42:02,960 --> 00:42:03,040
Yeah.

549
00:42:03,680 --> 00:42:03,760
Yeah.

550
00:42:04,400 --> 00:42:07,760
Any optical allusions, please send them my way.

551
00:42:07,920 --> 00:42:11,600
I love stuff like that that messes with perception, just like really captures you.

552
00:42:11,920 --> 00:42:19,760
So I think finding sources like that in print or digital art like that is really good inspiration for a black box.

553
00:42:20,160 --> 00:42:20,240
Yeah.

554
00:42:20,400 --> 00:42:36,640
I'm curious, like when you're designing new puzzles, obviously you're trying to come up with creative, you know, ways to use the sensors, but how would something like that come into play for like being inspirational for these types of things.

555
00:42:37,520 --> 00:42:39,680
Yeah, what do you mean visually or?

556
00:42:40,640 --> 00:42:53,280
Well, maybe that's maybe that's the answer is like, do you come up with a puzzle like, okay, I want to use this sensor and maybe we could maybe this is a technique for how the mechanic works and then you come up with a like, sort of design that goes with it?

557
00:42:53,520 --> 00:42:54,480
Yeah, it's a good question.

558
00:42:55,120 --> 00:42:58,720
I think I just realized I skipped your question earlier about having puzzle ideas.

559
00:42:59,200 --> 00:43:06,720
You were saying for AR, but I also have a very, very long list of puzzle ideas for the iPhone, which is probably infuriating to a lot of people.

560
00:43:07,360 --> 00:43:12,960
But a lot of those ideas, they need to be paired with, like, first of all, like, a puzzle idea.

561
00:43:12,960 --> 00:43:15,520
Like, what's a way to make that actually a fun challenge?

562
00:43:15,760 --> 00:43:18,240
It's not fun to find a setting and turn it on.

563
00:43:18,720 --> 00:43:19,920
There are some challenges like that.

564
00:43:21,120 --> 00:43:32,800
But it's if you can turn it into a game element, like anything that someone can change and like the app can observe that change, that's the way I think of it, like that can become a challenge, can be kind of gamified.

565
00:43:33,760 --> 00:43:37,520
But making something fun out of that is like step one and knowing that it's possible.

566
00:43:38,080 --> 00:43:40,160
And then step two is the visual.

567
00:43:40,400 --> 00:43:48,960
So like I have dozens of really cool ideas I would love to make for puzzles, but like I haven't thought of a game.

568
00:43:50,880 --> 00:43:58,960
There's like really obvious visuals that might be like iconographic or something, but that's not like a fun mind gap to cross.

569
00:43:59,520 --> 00:44:10,880
So coming up with something, it's often like a novel way to represent a sensor or some sort of interface thing that is somewhat familiar but not too familiar.

570
00:44:11,360 --> 00:44:15,280
Yeah, it has like they all seem like they all have like an abstract concept.

571
00:44:15,680 --> 00:44:15,760
Yeah.

572
00:44:16,240 --> 00:44:21,040
That does like it makes sense once you know what it's referring to.

573
00:44:21,360 --> 00:44:24,000
Yeah, that's exactly how it should work.

574
00:44:25,200 --> 00:44:32,000
It should make a lot of sense after, once you've connected that gap and like crossed that moat, so to speak.

575
00:44:32,880 --> 00:44:48,800
So that can be really difficult because I think we see mostly interfaces where people are trying to make it really painfully clear what's happening and this is not trying to do the opposite, but it's like trying to do something different.

576
00:44:49,120 --> 00:45:04,960
So often it has to be a completely novel interface and that's where I find it really useful to like hear about people solving other problems or seeing sort of these like solving things that are completely unrelated but in like simple geometric sort of design ways.

577
00:45:06,160 --> 00:45:15,840
And I really need to like think about this more and possibly try to write about it because I think there's some interesting nuggets in there but it's like not clear to explain.

578
00:45:16,960 --> 00:45:39,600
But there's ways of thinking about these sort of sensor inputs and like how you're interacting with them that sometimes inform how a graphic might loop because it's like sort of trying to convey some sense of like continuity or something like that or the way it uses color or like hints in its movement.

579
00:45:40,160 --> 00:45:42,240
I think there's like a lot of little things to grasp at.

580
00:45:42,400 --> 00:45:47,920
And that's why the moving Bees and Bombs GIFs and a lot of other interesting people like that.

581
00:45:48,640 --> 00:45:52,640
It's a good source of inspiration just to stare at and try to understand how it works.

582
00:45:53,200 --> 00:45:54,640
Yeah, that's fascinating.

583
00:45:55,520 --> 00:45:56,160
It's interesting.

584
00:45:56,720 --> 00:46:10,000
I feel like I wouldn't have thought this before, but now talking to you, I'm kind of realizing Blackbox is like it feels like it's weirdly straddling the line between a traditional game and a traditional app.

585
00:46:10,320 --> 00:46:27,200
I'm saying in quotes, but I guess a productivity app, I don't know what you call non game apps, but because yeah, it's like, like you said, an app, your whole goal is you want to make it very clear what the thing you need to do is or what a certain animation or movement or icon means.

586
00:46:27,840 --> 00:46:37,360
And you have this like this weird balance of at the end of the puzzle, once it's solved, you need that like delight feeling where it like you're like, oh, that does match.

587
00:46:37,600 --> 00:46:44,000
But then there's like a moment of frustration that's almost a requirement for the puzzle to work satisfyingly.

588
00:46:44,240 --> 00:46:44,320
Yeah.

589
00:46:44,640 --> 00:46:47,040
And like that has to be such a strange Yeah.

590
00:46:47,680 --> 00:46:50,960
design consideration to be making while you're building these?

591
00:46:51,280 --> 00:46:58,000
I think another one I just thought of is like it's kind of similar to comedians, that like aha moment.

592
00:46:58,400 --> 00:47:01,920
And I read something about this with like puns and why they're fun.

593
00:47:02,720 --> 00:47:06,800
And kind of it's like the self satisfaction of like putting together why it's funny.

594
00:47:09,040 --> 00:47:11,440
It's like it's not funny without that.

595
00:47:11,680 --> 00:47:14,960
Like you have to understand how it works, but like you're the one doing that work.

596
00:47:15,520 --> 00:47:18,160
And it's an interesting parallel.

597
00:47:18,320 --> 00:47:28,960
Like, yeah, you do want to create it something that isn't so frustrating that no one ever crosses that gap or it's not fun, but you don't want to make it so easy to walk across.

598
00:47:30,480 --> 00:47:33,200
Right, because the joy is in the work to solve it.

599
00:47:33,360 --> 00:47:34,480
Yeah, it's in that aha.

600
00:47:34,720 --> 00:47:48,400
And I think that's like a big part of Black Box's success is it doesn't just feel like you conquered something or like got to the next level, but it's like it really feels like you put in the work and like really figured something out.

601
00:47:48,560 --> 00:47:53,600
And it's it's kind of having 79 of those aha moments.

602
00:47:54,720 --> 00:47:57,040
It's a really good feeling that you would want to hopefully share.

603
00:47:57,280 --> 00:47:57,920
Yeah, definitely.

604
00:47:58,480 --> 00:47:59,360
Experience more things.

605
00:47:59,520 --> 00:48:00,800
If I could release more puzzles..

606
00:48:02,240 --> 00:48:04,000
There's plenty of other games that have this.

607
00:48:04,240 --> 00:48:06,560
I'll admit, I haven't completed Black Box.

608
00:48:07,680 --> 00:48:08,800
I'm one who gets frustrated.

609
00:48:09,040 --> 00:48:10,000
Maybe a little too easily.

610
00:48:10,240 --> 00:48:15,520
But part of me is now, like, I'm kind of excited to just go play a bunch and be looking at it through this lens now.

611
00:48:15,920 --> 00:48:17,280
So I'm excited for that.

612
00:48:18,000 --> 00:48:22,960
Yeah, turn on, turn on the sounds and don't be afraid to, the hints are tuned to not give everything away.

613
00:48:23,120 --> 00:48:24,480
Also, it's a big consideration.

614
00:48:24,800 --> 00:48:28,080
Yeah, I I'm not too proud to admit I've used the hints before.

615
00:48:28,320 --> 00:48:29,920
No, it's, it drives me nuts.

616
00:48:30,080 --> 00:48:32,480
I think like half of the people don't use hints and they're very proud of it.

617
00:48:32,720 --> 00:48:35,680
And then they eventually stop playing the game, which I think is less fun.

618
00:48:36,400 --> 00:48:36,480
Yeah.

619
00:48:38,240 --> 00:48:38,640
All right.

620
00:48:38,800 --> 00:48:42,960
Well, so where can people find you and find your game?

621
00:48:43,920 --> 00:48:44,080
Sure.

622
00:48:44,320 --> 00:49:01,200
I'm warplaying on everything and you can find Blackbox on the App Store or on blackboxpuzzles dot com dot Oh, and I, before we leave, I have to ask this question and I don't know why I skipped over to my notes, but who made that your little promo video because it is amazing.

623
00:49:01,680 --> 00:49:02,160
Which one?

624
00:49:02,880 --> 00:49:03,760
It's on your website.

625
